{
  "dom_id": "step-q106198",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The graph of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ with $a<0$ has discriminant $D<0.$ Which of the following could be the graph of this function?",
  "worked_solution": "To find the possible graph of the parabola, we need to determine two things:\n\n- The direction in which the parabola opens.\n\n- The number of distinct real roots of the quadratic function.\n\nSince $a<0,$ the parabola opens downward. Moreover, since the discriminant $D<0,$ the quadratic function has no real roots. So, it should not intersect the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only graph that satisfies both these conditions is the following:\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266874/assets/e77ecb2ee9c3b66db2dfc34f17a14641ad2c3a028a19225638ce37ae01ad49a9.png)",
